  "name": "Indonesian keroncong (Lusophone-derived urban folk)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Indonesian keroncong urban-folk genre emerging in sixteenth-seventeenth century Portuguese-trading-post communities of Tugu (north Jakarta) and Maluku from the Portuguese moresco and fado-precursor folk-music brought by Portuguese and Indo-Portuguese settlers, then absorbed into the Malay-Indonesian musical-cultural sphere over four centuries. Performance configuration centers on the cuk (small ukulele-like four-string lute) and cak (smaller four-string), plus violin, flute, cello (played pizzicato), and acoustic guitar. Vocal style features mid-tempo wide-vibrato male-or-female lead with characteristic Indonesian-Malay melodic-line aesthetic over Western-tonal-harmonic framework. The mid-twentieth-century Lokananta Records era anchored commercial-recording preservation.",
